een  relatively uninterested  but what happened was that we were able  to work on a relatively basic problem  that is the development of  of techniques  for isolation profusion of extremities  and other regions of the body and then  apply this to the treatment of human  cancer in a relatively short time that  is in a span of a relatively few months  we developed the techniques in dogs for  extracorporeal circulation and regional  perfusion and then applied it in the  treatment of human patients with cancer  the present medical officer in charge at  new orleans is dr johnny troutman who  has served at several public health  service hospitals and was formerly  director of the clinical center in  bethesda maryland  i considered  that research over the years in our  hospitals  was one of the best recruiting devices  that we could possibly have and i  believe that if the service is to go  forward and to attract  proper  personnel  career people to carry on the work of  the service  that the service  will have to  develop its research facilities in our  hospitals  to the utmost  we are leaders in the field of medicine  not only  in the united states but in the world  it behooves us  in the service  to have the best possible research  facilities  the best possible hospitals  to accomplish the aims which every good  clinical facility should have  when we began organizing this present  research program at our new orleans  hospital we were fortunate to have the  fullest possible cooperation and  encouragement from one of the nation's  leaders in medical education dr maxwell  lapham dean of tulane medical school  the close affiliation of the u.s public  service hospital with the school of  medicine of tulane university has many  important facets  in the first place it unifies two  institutions  uh which are dedicated to medical  training and medical care  it allows for the interchange of staff  and facilities specifically the  facilities of the public health service  hospital 